<article class="reader">
  <h1>how we work.</h1>
  <p class="reader__lede">The first engagement delivers a Design Plan: a proposed product direction, the decisions behind it, and the detail your team needs to build.</p>
</article>

<div class="section-break">
  <span class="section-break__num">II</span>
  <span>GETTING STARTED</span>
</div>

<article class="reader">
  <h2>agree on the work.</h2>
  <p>We begin with a conversation about your product, the people using it, and the decision you need to make. A proposal defines the scope, deliverables, fee, and schedule for a Design Plan.</p>
  <p>We agree on access to users and domain experts, who will review the work, and any technical or business constraints. Those agreements shape the engagement.</p>
</article>

<div class="section-break">
  <span class="section-break__num">III</span>
  <span>THE DESIGN WORK</span>
</div>

<article class="reader">
  <h2>understand and model the product.</h2>
  <p>We review the available evidence and talk with the people who know the work. We map what the product needs to represent, how those things relate, and what people need to do with them.</p>
  <p>Together, we define the opportunity and priorities. Assumptions and unanswered questions stay visible throughout.</p>

  <h2>design and review the journeys.</h2>
  <p>We explore design directions, compare their tradeoffs, and develop the selected approach into flows and wireframes. Each screen has a job, including when information is missing, an action fails, or access is limited.</p>
  <p>You review the work in the browser. We check that the screens, requirements, and product model agree, and define how to test the remaining assumptions.</p>
</article>

<div class="section-break">
  <span class="section-break__num">IV</span>
  <span>YOUR DESIGN PLAN</span>
</div>

<article class="reader">
  <h2>review the direction before building.</h2>
  <p>Your Design Plan brings the work into one reviewable set:</p>
  <ol class="needs-list--expanded">
    <li>
      <span class="step">01</span>
      <h3 class="step-title">context and decisions.</h3>
      <p class="step-body">The opportunity, goals, evidence, scope, and reasons for the chosen direction.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step">02</span>
      <h3 class="step-title">the product model.</h3>
      <p class="step-body">Object guides that define the product’s key things, their information, relationships, and actions.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step">03</span>
      <h3 class="step-title">journeys and wireframes.</h3>
      <p class="step-body">The key tasks, proposed screens, interactions, and alternate paths.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="step">04</span>
      <h3 class="step-title">requirements and next tests.</h3>
      <p class="step-body">What the product must do, how to check it, and what still needs validation.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p><a href="{{ '/#design-plans' | url }}" class="forward-cue">Explore Design Plans</a></p>
</article>

<div class="section-break">
  <span class="section-break__num">V</span>
  <span>WHAT COMES NEXT</span>
</div>

<article class="reader">
  <h2>build from an agreed plan.</h2>
  <p>Once you accept the Design Plan, your team can use it to scope production. You can also discuss a separate build with ReShaped, priced from the accepted plan.</p>
  <p><a href="{{ site.contactAnchor | url }}" class="forward-cue">Start a conversation</a></p>
</article>

